WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.990
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and
today I'm going to talk to you about

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:06.690
Atomic Habits and language learning.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.120
If you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:12.360
Click on the bell for notifications,
and if you follow me on a

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.400
podcast service, leave a comment.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.210
I do appreciate it.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.440
So Atomic Habits is a book
that I recently bought.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.600
I bought it for a variety of reasons.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.800
I bought one copy each
for my two grandsons.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.270
The girls got other books.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:32.325
Uh, I have a brother who is in,
uh, eastern Canada and he has been

00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:36.705
bedridden and he has to gradually
train himself to get outta bed

00:00:36.705 --> 00:00:38.355
and start using his muscles more.

00:00:38.445 --> 00:00:44.655
It's a long road, so I bought him
an audiobook version and I bought

00:00:44.655 --> 00:00:47.085
myself an audiobook version,
and I've been listening to it.

00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:52.485
And here's what I think is,
is very valuable in the book

00:00:52.605 --> 00:00:55.065
Atomic Habits by James Clear.

00:00:55.275 --> 00:00:59.655
By the way, I will leave a link to a
summary of the book, which the author

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.415
himself has put up on the internet.

00:01:02.745 --> 00:01:06.105
And there are also, if you Google, you'll
find lots of references to this book.

00:01:07.095 --> 00:01:14.955
In essence, what the author demonstrates
and illustrates is the fact that if

00:01:14.955 --> 00:01:18.840
you wanna achieve something, it's
not enough just to think in terms of

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.149
the goal: I want to be rich or, uh,
you know, my brother might be saying,

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:28.890
I want to be walking again soon, or
for language learning, I want to be

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.780
fluent in such and such a language.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:36.750
What matters much more than your sort
of long-term goal, your ambition, what

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.550
matters more is what you do every day.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.470
And he uses a number of examples
early in the book to illustrate how

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.755
very small actions if repeated, have
tremendous effect over the long haul.

00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:54.885
Uh, he mentions, for example, that he
himself was in an accident where he

00:01:54.885 --> 00:02:00.375
was hit by a baseball bat in a game
and it damaged his eye, his nose, uh,

00:02:00.435 --> 00:02:02.265
maybe even his cognitive functions.

00:02:02.865 --> 00:02:07.425
And by dint of working at it very,
very persistently, over two years,

00:02:07.425 --> 00:02:11.655
he fully regained his ability to
play baseball and has done very well.

00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:17.100
Uh, he mentions an example of
the British uh, cycling team,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.420
which had never done well.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.980
And then they decided to apply
the principle of 1% improvement on

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.120
all the things that they did from
diet, to training, to equipment

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.640
to where they slept and, and, and
where they studied and you name it.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.019
And the net result was a significant 30
odd percent improvement, or at least,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:41.010
uh, they started winning championships
and the, the Tour de France and so forth.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.260
Uh, he also gives the example of an
airplane in which leaving, say the, uh,

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.740
an airport on the east coast heading
towards the west coast of the United

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:55.200
States, if it's even a few degrees
off its flight plan, by the time it

00:02:55.205 --> 00:02:58.770
reaches its destination, it'll be
hundreds of miles off its destination.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.850
So there's this tremendous
cumulative effect of small actions.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:07.770
What this means is, if you want
to get somewhere, it's not just

00:03:07.775 --> 00:03:12.420
about where you want to get it's,
it's what you are doing every day.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:16.140
To what extent are you taking
advantage of the time that you have?

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.420
To what extent do you have habits
that are gonna help you move in the

00:03:21.424 --> 00:03:22.920
direction that you want to get to?

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:28.709
And, um, you know, I've certainly felt
this when it comes to language learning.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.200
Uh, at LingQ for example, we don't measure
what you have achieved in the language

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.510
because that's to some extent subjective.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.820
We measure your level of activity.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:43.019
If you get active, if you spend time
with the language, you will improve.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:48.209
If you are active and if you have
positive habits, you will work towards

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.040
whatever goal you want to achieve.

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:55.200
And the other thing, by the way,
about Atomic Habits is, uh, it's

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.780
sort of a new year's resolution time.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.940
So a lot of people would like to, uh, you
know, get more fit or learn a language

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.550
or, uh, you know, uh, improve their
eating habits or whatever it might be.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:13.470
So it's very timely to, to have
a book that gives you some ideas

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.900
on how to develop better habits.

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:23.700
So, and he talks about then focusing on
your habits, actions, repeated actions

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.890
that you can do, but regularly that
will improve you more than some very

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.130
ambitious goal or nebulous motivation.

00:04:32.775 --> 00:04:36.315
And he has this sort of pattern
when it comes to habits.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:39.315
He says to develop a new habit,
and I'm going from memory, I may

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:42.165
not remember it all, but you need
to have something that triggers,

00:04:42.405 --> 00:04:44.565
triggers the desire to do something.

00:04:45.015 --> 00:04:49.725
So the trigger, uh, and then
that's followed up by the craving.

00:04:49.725 --> 00:04:56.219
So maybe you suddenly feel your stomach
and there's a kind of a, a roll of fat

00:04:56.219 --> 00:05:00.540
there, the result of eating too much
food over Christmas, that's a trigger.

00:05:00.659 --> 00:05:01.770
You then have a craving.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.210
I want to go and work out.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.780
Uh, then that whatever has...

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:12.659
you have, you are now craving to do
it has to be sort of easy for you.

00:05:12.659 --> 00:05:14.520
It has to be something
you can actually do.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.440
So you can actually go to the gym.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:21.420
Uh, and then there has to be a sense
of satisfaction once you've done it.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:26.790
Uh, each time you go to the gym, you feel
good about yourself, and that is in itself

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:32.340
enough satisfaction to keep you going so
that you have this sort of cue or trigger

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.680
followed by, uh, some kind of something
about it that's appealing or attractive.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:38.970
It's not enough to be triggered.

00:05:38.975 --> 00:05:40.110
You have to wanna do it.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.950
Uh, and then you have the response,
what do you actually do about it?

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.660
And then you have the reward.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:50.190
If all of that falls into place, you
will gradually develop that as a habit.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.580
He says a number of things that are
very interesting in the book, and I

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.380
really recommend getting the book.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.040
He also says, you know, start
off by doing something easy.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.290
He talks about a two minute activity.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.850
Uh, so again, uh, I have said
this before in one of my videos.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:10.050
Uh, you know, when you're going
through a new text, don't worry

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:16.170
about all of the words that you can't
remember or, or don't understand,

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.460
or that you don't understand all.

00:06:18.305 --> 00:06:19.785
Go through it once half understanding.

00:06:19.785 --> 00:06:20.565
Go through it again.

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:21.315
Go through it again.

00:06:21.525 --> 00:06:25.185
As long as you have an activity
which is pleasant and where you

00:06:25.185 --> 00:06:29.325
have a sense of achievement, uh,
don't have to bite off too much.

00:06:29.775 --> 00:06:36.945
And sometimes if you'll just start in
any activity that will lead you to maybe

00:06:36.945 --> 00:06:39.255
something else or lead you to continue.

00:06:39.375 --> 00:06:41.715
But the important thing is to get started.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:47.950
He also talks in his book about this um,
I can't remember what term he used, but

00:06:47.969 --> 00:06:53.880
you can be engaged in these activities and
not see any results for the longest time,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:55.859
and then there is sort of a tipping point.

00:06:56.099 --> 00:07:00.510
There can come a tipping point where
all of a sudden the cumulative effect

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:05.310
of these improvements or of these
actions or this habit is going to show

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:09.499
it itself in terms of, you know, all of
a sudden pushing you towards your goal.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.925
I'm not sure that I agree
with that all the time.

00:07:11.925 --> 00:07:15.405
I think there's a, a
gradual cumulative effect.

00:07:15.645 --> 00:07:18.525
It's just that we don't
feel that improvement.

00:07:18.945 --> 00:07:21.705
Uh, and I've often said, you
know, when it comes to language

00:07:21.705 --> 00:07:23.595
learning, trust your brain.

00:07:23.625 --> 00:07:27.195
If you are putting in the time, if
you are listening, if you are reading

00:07:27.525 --> 00:07:33.105
even small amounts, that is gradually
improving you, the brain is getting

00:07:33.105 --> 00:07:35.015
a better handle on the language.

00:07:35.795 --> 00:07:37.920
I'm not sure that when it comes
to language learning, there's

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:42.690
this sudden moment when we sort of
all of a sudden jump to fluency.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:47.400
I think it's a constant continuous
effort, but every little bit we do,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.080
and I think this is an important point
in the book, um, you know, if it's

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.610
only five minutes, do five minutes.

00:07:53.615 --> 00:07:55.830
If it's 10 minutes, do the 10 minutes.

00:07:56.039 --> 00:07:59.940
Don't let a day go by without
doing something, because over

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.000
time the cumulative effect of
small actions is very large.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.130
And that's another reason why at
LingQ we have these, uh, we have

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:11.520
these LingQ, at least these uh,
streaks that you can maintain.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.430
So there's some motivation
having set a goal.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.670
He also talks about the importance
of measuring your activity.

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:21.690
So if you set yourself a goal, I'm
gonna read so many words, or save so

00:08:21.695 --> 00:08:23.670
many words, or listen to so many hours.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:29.560
Then, and then we have the sort of
composite measure, which is the coins, and

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.320
if you commit yourself to a certain level
of activity, then you'll wanna maintain

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.510
that level of, of activity every day.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:40.250
And it's better to have a lower
level of activity and maintain

00:08:40.255 --> 00:08:44.340
that rather than sort of shooting
your bolt, uh, one day and then not

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.870
doing anything for two, three days.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:50.490
Uh, again, it's, it's all about these,
you know, taking advantage of the time

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:58.530
you have during the day, uh, to, um,
commit yourself to positive, uh, habit.

00:08:58.965 --> 00:09:03.735
That cumulative cumulatively
are going to, you know, impel

00:09:03.735 --> 00:09:05.925
you towards your, uh, objective.

00:09:06.945 --> 00:09:09.165
He talks about layers of ac...

00:09:09.255 --> 00:09:13.455
of, of habits, and in particular
he focuses on the importance

00:09:13.515 --> 00:09:16.905
of your identity habits.

00:09:17.535 --> 00:09:22.995
Uh, he uses the example of someone
who says they wanna quit smoking or

00:09:23.055 --> 00:09:26.205
wants to quit smoking, and someone
says to them, have another cigarette.

00:09:26.355 --> 00:09:28.025
One person says, I'm trying to quit.

00:09:28.655 --> 00:09:30.630
The other person says, I'm not a smoker.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:36.000
So that's a much more positive
habit to have in terms of your, uh,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:38.040
you know, identity of who you are.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.450
So if you're learning a language, you
don't say, I'm trying to learn a language.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:44.520
I am a language learner.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:51.390
I am a person who learns to speak other
languages to a high degree of proficiency.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:52.620
I am a language learner.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.755
To have that habit built around your
identity of, of who you are is going to

00:09:58.755 --> 00:10:03.825
make you more positive, more confident,
uh, and keep you on track, I think,

00:10:04.245 --> 00:10:07.745
uh, better than just saying, well, I'm
kind of trying to learn this language.

00:10:08.235 --> 00:10:12.555
Um, so there are, I'm just touching
on some of the points in the book.

00:10:13.064 --> 00:10:15.375
Um, I think it's a valuable book.

00:10:15.944 --> 00:10:19.545
Uh, I don't do it justice in,
in the sort of things that I'm

00:10:19.545 --> 00:10:21.285
able to remember about the book.

00:10:21.885 --> 00:10:26.025
Uh, but when we come to language
learning, we not only need to

00:10:26.145 --> 00:10:30.345
train ourselves in the habit of
doing a little something every day.

00:10:30.375 --> 00:10:34.275
Even it's a small, even if it's a small
amount, we need to train ourselves

00:10:34.275 --> 00:10:38.865
in the habit of, of recognizing that
every little bit we do, listening,

00:10:38.865 --> 00:10:42.675
reading, speaking, writing is
moving us towards our objective.

00:10:42.915 --> 00:10:48.255
Even if we're not conscious of the
fact that we are improving, we are.

00:10:48.585 --> 00:10:54.435
And the cumulative effect of small
actions is very large, both in terms of

00:10:54.825 --> 00:10:58.770
language and in terms of other things
in life as he explains in the book.

00:10:59.099 --> 00:11:04.110
So we need to develop those, uh,
learning habits, but we also need to

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:08.160
train our brains in new language habits.

00:11:08.849 --> 00:11:12.930
And, uh, so we sort of, uh, as
language learners, we have sort of

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:17.130
two aspects of, of habits that would
need, we need to develop our learning

00:11:17.135 --> 00:11:24.660
habits, our attitudinal habits, our
uh, self-identity as language, as

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.010
language learners, those habits.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:31.199
But at the same time, we're helping our
brain develop new language habits so that

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.031
patterns in the new language vocabulary,
in the new language structures, in

00:11:36.036 --> 00:11:39.870
the new language pronunciation, the
new, new language, all of which are

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:43.439
different from our native language
or other languages that we speak.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.500
We need to train our
brain in these new habits.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:53.505
And just as with other, the cumulative
effect of small actions is very large,

00:11:53.715 --> 00:11:59.025
and we just have to believe that if we
persist, if we maintain our activity,

00:11:59.025 --> 00:12:04.545
the brain will develop the language
habits that are necessary in order to

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:06.945
become comfortable in a new language.

00:12:07.185 --> 00:12:11.985
So there you have it, atomic habits
as applied to language learning.

00:12:12.375 --> 00:12:15.795
I have talked about habits before as
it applies to language learning, so

00:12:15.795 --> 00:12:17.075
I'll leave you with a couple of videos.

00:12:17.285 --> 00:12:18.405
Thank you for listening.

00:12:18.515 --> 00:12:19.045
Bye for now.

